    $astroCmp_master$> Unit I explained how the worlds were made. Unit II examines what they *became* — and it opens not with a planet but with a **framework**, because the single most important decision in studying the terrestrial worlds is whether to treat them as five separate subjects or as **five outcomes of the same physics under different conditions.** Mercury, Venus, Earth, the Moon, and Mars formed from the same disk, inside the same snow line, from essentially the same materials, under the same laws — yet one is a scorched airless rock, one an oven under a crushing atmosphere, one a frozen desert, one a dead cratered world, and one is covered in oceans and life. **Something turned identical ingredients into radically divergent outcomes, and comparative planetology is the discipline of finding out what.** We will build five tools — interiors, heat, surfaces, atmospheres, and magnetic fields — and one of them, the geometry of how a planet loses heat, will turn out to organize the entire unit. Master these five and the lectures that follow stop being descriptions of individual worlds and become a controlled experiment in planetary physics.

## The day the planets became places

On 14 July 1965 a small spacecraft called **Mariner 4** flew past Mars, took **twenty-one photographs**, stored them on tape, and spent days transmitting them back across 220 million kilometres — so slowly that engineers hand-coloured the first image with pastels before the computer rendering was ready. The pictures were grainy and covered barely 1% of the surface. They were also one of the great disillusionments in astronomy, and simultaneously the birth of a science.

For a century Mars had been the most *imagined* world in the solar system — Percival Lowell's canals, a dying civilization irrigating a desiccating planet; and even after the canals were discredited, most expected Mars to be broadly Earth-like, with seasons and perhaps vegetation. What Mariner 4 showed was **craters.** Heavily cratered, ancient, lunar-looking terrain, under an atmosphere less than one percent of Earth's pressure. No canals, no oceans. Mars looked disconcertingly like the Moon.

{{image: Mariner 4 | One of the twenty-one photographs Mariner 4 returned as it flew past Mars in 1965 — grainy, low-resolution, and revolutionary. Instead of the canals and vegetation a century of astronomers had imagined, it showed a heavily cratered, ancient, lunar-looking surface under a wisp of an atmosphere. The public was disappointed; science was transformed. Before Mariner 4, planets were points of light with orbits and spectra. After it, they were places, with geology — and the craters proved the same processes shape every world.}}

The public was disappointed; the scientific consequence was profound. **Before Mariner 4, planets were astronomical objects — points of light with orbits and spectra. After it, they were *places*, with geology.** And the craters showed that the *same processes* operate across the solar system: the Moon is cratered, Mars is cratered, Mercury is cratered. Impacts are universal — and if impacts are universal, so presumably are volcanism, tectonics, and atmospheric escape. **The physics that shapes one world shapes all of them.** That realization founded **comparative planetology**, which treats the terrestrial worlds not as a collection of curiosities but as a set of natural experiments. And the experiment is favourable: the five bodies formed from the same disk, from the same materials, under the same laws, differing in only a few parameters — chiefly **size**, **distance from the Sun**, and the accidents of their histories. Yet the outcomes range from airless rock to runaway oven to living ocean. **Five worlds, same rules, radically different fates. Why?** Let us start beneath the surface.

## Interiors: differentiation, and how we see inside

A body assembled from a jumble of rock, metal, and ice does not stay mixed if it melts. Once material becomes fluid, **density takes over**: iron and nickel sink, silicates rise, the lightest material floats to the top, and the body separates into **core, mantle, crust** — a process called **differentiation.** It needs heat, and the early solar system supplied it generously; and it is self-reinforcing, because dropping an iron core through a planet's radius releases enormous gravitational energy as heat, promoting further melting. Once started in a body of sufficient size, it runs to completion. All five worlds are differentiated, though to differing degrees — Mercury's core is proportionally enormous, the Moon's tiny.

We cannot cut a planet open, but we read its interior four ways, each invoked in the chapters ahead. **Mean density** — mass over volume: Mercury's 5,427 kg/m³, nearly Earth's despite being far smaller and less self-compressed, immediately implies an outsized iron core. **Moment of inertia** — how mass is distributed radially, measured through spin-axis precession and gravity, distinguishing a concentrated core from a uniform body. **Gravity mapping** — a spacecraft's orbit is perturbed by density variations beneath it, revealing crustal thickness and buried masses (NASA's GRAIL did this for the Moon). And **seismology**, the gold standard: seismic waves' speeds and paths depend on the material they cross, and crucially **S-waves cannot pass through liquid**, casting a shadow — which is how Earth's liquid outer core was found. We have now done seismology on three worlds: Earth, the Moon (Apollo left seismometers running for years), and Mars, where NASA's InSight lander confirmed a large liquid core.

## Heat: the master variable

Here is the single most important idea in the unit. **A planet is a heat engine.** Nearly everything geologically interesting a world does — volcanism, tectonics, mountain-building, generating a magnetic field — is powered by internal heat escaping to space. So a planet's geological life story is the story of **how much heat it has and how fast it loses it.** The heat comes from four sources: **accretional heating** (impacting planetesimals converting gravitational energy to heat as the body grows — strongly size-dependent), **differentiation** (iron sinking), **radioactive decay** (long-lived uranium, thorium, and potassium power Earth's interior today; short-lived aluminium-26 melted bodies in the first few million years — which is why even 500-km Vesta is differentiated), and **tidal heating** (flexing by a nearby massive body — negligible for our five worlds but dominant for some outer moons).

Now the crucial part, and it is beautifully simple. **Heat is generated throughout a body's volume**, which scales as $R^3$; **heat is lost through its surface**, which scales as $R^2$. So the ratio of loss capacity to heat content is

$$\frac{A}{V} = \frac{4\pi R^2}{\frac{4}{3}\pi R^3} = \frac{3}{R}$$

**Small bodies have proportionally far more surface per unit volume, and cool far faster. Large bodies stay hot.** It is why a cup of coffee cools faster than a bathtub — elementary geometry, and in planetary science it is decisive. Explore how the worlds' cooling rates and geological fates line up:

```coolingsize
```

The correlation is essentially perfect: order the terrestrial worlds by size and you have ordered them by geological vitality. The two smallest are dead, the intermediate one mostly dead with a vigorous past, the two largest active. This is not a coincidence — **it is the organizing principle of Unit II.** When we ask why Mercury has no volcanoes, why the Moon's face has not changed since before complex life existed on Earth, why Mars's volcanoes are extinct, and ultimately why Mars is dead while Earth is alive, the first term in every answer is the same: **how big is it?** Because size sets cooling rate, cooling rate sets how long a world stays alive, and geological life governs atmospheres, magnetic fields, and habitability. **Distance from the Sun was the master variable of Unit I. Size is the master variable of Unit II.**

```example
surface-volume
```

## Surfaces: volcanism, tectonics, and why Earth is unique

Internal heat has to get out, and it writes itself across a planet's surface through two channels. **Volcanism** — molten rock reaching the surface — is the most direct, and it is *universal*: every one of our worlds shows volcanic terrain, including the Moon (its dark *maria* are flood-basalt plains) and Mercury. It matters beyond geology, because it is the principal source of **secondary atmospheres** — the water vapour, carbon dioxide, nitrogen, and sulphur compounds outgassed from a planet's interior, since the terrestrial planets never captured primordial hydrogen envelopes. So the size argument has an atmospheric edge: **a world that cools quickly stops outgassing early**, losing the ability to replenish an atmosphere it is also losing to space. Hold that for Mars.

**Tectonics** — motion of the outer shell — comes in two regimes, and the distinction is central. **Stagnant-lid tectonics** is the normal case, on Mercury, the Moon, Mars, and (modified) Venus: a single unbroken rigid shell, beneath which the mantle may convect, but crust is not recycled — heat escapes by conduction and volcanism, and surfaces deform (Mercury's cooling contraction made kilometre-high thrust scarps) without recycling. **Plate tectonics** occurs on **Earth alone**, so far as we know: the shell is broken into rigid plates that move, creating crust at spreading ridges and destroying it at subduction zones, recycling the entire ocean floor every ~200 million years. **Why is Earth unique?** The leading answer is **water**, which weakens silicate rock — lowering the lithosphere's strength, lubricating faults, and promoting melting where it is carried down at subduction zones. Without water, rock is too strong for the shell to break, and it stays a stagnant lid.

The comparison with **Venus** is pointed. Venus is nearly Earth's twin in size, so it has comparable internal heat — but it is **bone dry**, and it has *no plate tectonics*; instead it appears to have undergone a catastrophic **global resurfacing** ~500 million years ago, plausibly because a stagnant lid trapped heat until the whole surface failed at once. **Two planets of the same size, one wet and one dry, with completely different tectonic styles** — comparative planetology working as intended, isolating a single variable. And the consequences run deep: on Earth, plate tectonics drives the **carbon–silicate cycle** that regulates atmospheric CO₂ over geological time and has kept the climate stable for billions of years. Venus has no such thermostat.

## Cratering as a clock: dating a world you have never touched

Now the most useful *practical* tool in the field. **Impact craters let us date surfaces on worlds we have never sampled.** Impacts accumulate, so **more craters per unit area means an older surface; fewer craters means a younger one**, resurfaced by lava, tectonics, or erosion and its count reset. This gives *relative* ages anywhere we have images: the Moon's bright **highlands** are saturated with craters, while the dark **maria** have roughly a tenth the density — so the maria are much younger lava plains that flooded older terrain.

{{image: Moon | The near side of the Moon, its bright, heavily cratered highlands giving way to the dark, smooth maria. That contrast is a clock. The highlands, saturated with craters, are ancient — about 4.4 billion years old; the maria, with roughly a tenth the crater density, are younger lava plains that flooded and erased the older terrain, dating to 3.1–3.9 billion years. Because Apollo returned dated rocks from locations whose craters had been counted, this crater-density clock is calibrated in absolute years — and extended, with care, to Mercury, Mars, and moons no one has ever sampled.}}

Relative ages are useful; absolute ages are transformative, and here Apollo did something invaluable. It returned samples **from locations whose crater densities had been measured**, and radiometric dating of those samples, paired with the counts, yields a **calibration curve** of crater density versus absolute age. For the Moon we can now say the highlands are ~4.4 billion years old and the maria ~3.1–3.9 — from dated rocks, not theory. And then the leap: **that calibration extends, with care, to other worlds** — adjusting for differences in impact flux (bodies near Jupiter's gravity get hit more) and surface gravity — so we can estimate ages for surfaces on **Mercury, Mars, and the outer moons**, none ever sampled. **We can look at a photograph of a Martian plain, count the craters, and state its age in billions of years.** The method has honest caveats — it rests on the Apollo samples with their nearside sampling bias, extrapolating the flux adds uncertainty, secondary craters can inflate counts, and saturated surfaces can only be dated as "very old" — but it is the backbone of solar-system chronology.

## Atmospheres: acquiring, keeping, and losing them

An atmosphere is not a permanent possession — it must be **acquired**, **retained** against continuous loss, and the balance explains most of what distinguishes our worlds at the surface. Terrestrial atmospheres are **secondary**: never having captured hydrogen envelopes, these planets acquired their air afterward, by **outgassing** (volcanism releasing trapped volatiles — dependent, again, on staying geologically active) and **late delivery** (impacts by comets and water-rich asteroids). What a planet *keeps* is decided by a lovely competition between two velocities. Gas molecules have a thermal speed $v_{th} = \sqrt{3kT/m}$ — hotter gas moves faster, and at a given temperature lighter molecules move faster than heavy ones. Escaping requires exceeding $v_{esc} = \sqrt{2GM/R}$. The subtlety is that a molecule need not have *average* speed above escape velocity: the Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution has a high-speed tail, and those molecules leak away (**Jeans escape**), the tail continually repopulated by collisions. The rule of thumb is that a planet retains a gas over the age of the solar system if $v_{esc} \gtrsim 6\,v_{th}$. Test it:

```example
atmospheric-retention
```

Read across the worlds and a great deal falls out. **Mercury** is small *and* hot — the worst combination — and holds essentially nothing. **The Moon** is small and warm; same result. **Mars** is small but *cold*, which partly compensates, so it *should* keep CO₂ and N₂ — that it has so little anyway points to a *different* loss mechanism, solar-wind stripping, which is why Mars needs the magnetic-field story. **Earth** retains everything except hydrogen and helium — which is why our air holds essentially no free hydrogen despite it being the most abundant element in the universe: we cannot hold it. And **Titan** is the beautiful case: *smaller than Mercury*, escape velocity only 2.64 km/s, yet it keeps a nitrogen atmosphere *thicker than Earth's* — because at 94 K its molecules crawl so slowly that even feeble gravity suffices. **Temperature matters as much as gravity.**

Finally, what an atmosphere *does* to surface temperature. Sunlight passes through a transparent atmosphere and warms the ground, which re-radiates in the **infrared** — and gases like CO₂ and water vapour absorb infrared and send part back down, so the surface must warm until it can force enough energy out to balance the incoming light. That warming is the **greenhouse effect**, and it produces this block's most counterintuitive result:

```greenhouse
```

Study that. **Venus's equilibrium temperature is 227 K — *colder* than Earth's 254 K.** Venus is closer to the Sun and receives 1.9 times the flux, but its brilliant cloud reflects **77%** of incoming sunlight against Earth's 31%, so on the energy it actually absorbs Venus should be the *cooler* planet. Its surface is **737 K** — hotter than Mercury's dayside, despite being nearly twice as far from the Sun.

```example
greenhouse-temp
```

> **Venus is not hot because it is close to the Sun. It is hot because of its atmosphere — by 510 kelvin.**

That is the single most important fact about Venus. Meanwhile Earth's modest **+34 K** is exactly what lifts our mean temperature from a frozen 254 K to a habitable 288 K — **the greenhouse effect is not a pathology; it is a prerequisite for our existence.** What went wrong on Venus was only its magnitude.

{{image: Venus | Venus, wrapped in the brilliant, unbroken white cloud that reflects 77% of the sunlight reaching it — more than any other planet. That high reflectivity means Venus actually absorbs less energy per square metre than Earth does, so its equilibrium temperature is a mere 227 K, colder than Earth's 254 K. Yet beneath the clouds its surface sits at 737 K, hot enough to melt lead, because its massive carbon-dioxide atmosphere traps outgoing heat by more than five hundred degrees. Venus is not hot because it is close to the Sun; it is hot because of its air — the greenhouse effect run to catastrophe.}}

## Magnetic fields: the dynamo and the shield

The last tool. We saw that the solar wind strips unprotected atmospheres and that magnetic fields deflect it — so what does a planet need to *have* a field? A field is generated by a **dynamo**, and it requires **three conditions at once**: an electrically **conducting fluid layer** (a liquid iron core, for terrestrial planets); **convection** in that layer, which requires heat still flowing out of the core; and **rotation**, whose Coriolis effect organizes the convection into a coherent, ordered field. **Lose any one and the field dies** — and notice that the second requirement links straight back to size and cooling.

Two of our worlds deserve comment. **Mars is the crucial case.** Its crustal rocks are magnetized, which *proves* it had a global field early and then lost it — and the chain is exactly the one this framework promises: **Mars is small → it cooled fast → its core stopped convecting → the dynamo died → the solar wind reached its atmosphere → the atmosphere was stripped → surface water could no longer persist → Mars became a frozen desert.** A planet's *size* determined its *habitability*, through a chain of physics. **Venus is the puzzle**: Earth-sized, so it should have plenty of heat, yet it has no field. Its 243-day rotation is often blamed, but the likelier explanation is the convection requirement — without plate tectonics to carry heat efficiently out of the mantle, Venus's core may not be cooling fast enough to convect. If so, **the absence of plate tectonics and the absence of a magnetic field are connected**, and both trace to the absence of water. A hypothesis, not a settled result — but it shows how the tools interlock.

## The framework assembled

Gather the toolkit. **Mariner 4** made planets into places and founded the comparative method: the same processes operate on every world, so the five terrestrial bodies — same disk, same materials, same laws, differing chiefly in **size** and **distance** — are close to a controlled experiment. **Tool 1, interiors:** bodies that get hot enough **differentiate** into core, mantle, and crust, read through mean density, moment of inertia, gravity mapping, and seismology. **Tool 2, heat — the master variable:** a planet is a heat engine, and because heat is made in the volume ($R^3$) but lost through the surface ($R^2$), the ratio $3/R$ means small worlds cool fast and die geologically young — and the observed vitality of the five worlds tracks their size essentially perfectly. **Tool 3, surfaces:** heat escapes by universal volcanism and by tectonics, which is stagnant-lid everywhere except Earth, where water enables plate tectonics — and crater counting gives an absolute clock, calibrated by Apollo. **Tool 4, atmospheres:** secondary, outgassed, retained by the race $v_{esc} \gtrsim 6 v_{th}$ (so cold little Titan out-holds Mars), and warmed by a greenhouse effect that makes Venus's 227 K equilibrium into a 737 K surface. **Tool 5, magnetic fields:** a dynamo needs a conducting fluid, convection, and rotation, with convection tied back to size and cooling.

**Here is what to take from this chapter.** Consider the sentence "Mars has no magnetic field." As an isolated fact, it is trivia. Within this framework, it is a link in a chain that runs from a planet's *size* all the way to its *habitability* — small, so fast-cooling, so a stalled core, so a dead dynamo, so a stripped atmosphere, so no surface water, so a frozen desert. **That is what a framework buys you: facts become consequences.** And it works forward as well as backward — these same tools will let us reason about Titan, Europa, and eventually planets orbiting other stars that no one will ever visit. **Learn the framework, and the worlds largely explain themselves. Next: Mercury, the stripped world — and the puzzle of why it is made mostly of iron.**$astroCmp_master$,
